  "schema:datePublished": "2020-05-08",
  "schema:dateModified": "2020-05-08",
  "schema:inLanguage": "ENGLISH",
  "schema:description": "This publication presents a set of occurrence records of bird species observed in Arabuko Sokoke Forest in coastal Kenya between June 2012 and May 2013. The survey conducted lists 1264 records all bird individuals observed and their geo-reference positions within the primary forest, neigbouring plantation forest and farmland. Publication of this data adds to the global database on the avian community to advance research on avian species distribution modeling, for the understanding of the effect of land use and climate change on the avian community at local, regional and global scales.\n\nProject: The effects of land use on diversity and abundance of avian pollinators and frugivores in coastal ecosystems\n\nProject abstract: IFS D/5125-1\n\nStudy area: Arabuko Sokoke Forest (ASF) is the largest area of coastal forest remaining in East Africa and a major Important Bird Area in mainland Kenya. Arabuko Sokoke Forest (ASF), with a total area of approximately 41 600 ha, is the largest surviving single block of the previously extensive indigenous coastal tropical forest in Eastern Africa.\n\nStudy design: Species data prepared in as a CSV file aligned to Darwin Core Standard (DwC) of preparing biodiversity occurrence data and published through the IPT at the National Museums of Kenya.",
